  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Multisectoral Support to Enhance Integration of Ukrainians and Third Country Nationals in Poland</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Russia&#x2019;s full-scale invasion of Ukraine, which began on 24 February 2022, has led to a humanitarian and refugee crisis on a scale unprecedented in the region since World War II. Poland hosted one of the largest Ukrainian migrant populations in Europe before 2022. Since 24 February 2022, more than 6.8 million people have entered Poland from Ukraine. According to the key findings of the needs assessments conducted by the Displacement Tracking Matrix (DTM), while most respondents hope to return to Ukraine one day, for the time being, the majority plan to stay in Poland. Russia&#x2019;s intensified attacks on the critical civilian energy infrastructure in Ukraine since October are expected to lead to new population outflows from the country during wintertime. Accommodation and employment were highlighted as two major and immediate needs reported by refugees, followed by in-kind assistance and healthcare. Addressing the priority needs of refugees will help support their socio-economic inclusion in host countries, mitigate against protection risks and potentially dangerous coping mechanisms, and contribute to the overall integration into host communities. Through this project, IOM is providing multi-sectoral interventions focused on humanitarian assistance to vulnerable displaced and conflict-affected populations from Ukraine, with a focus on humanitarian and protection assistance.</narrative>
  </description>
